LSBLK(8)                     System Administration                    LSBLK(8)



NNAAMMEE
       lsblk - list block devices

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       llssbbllkk [options] [_d_e_v_i_c_e...]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       llssbbllkk lists information about all available or the specified block
       devices. The llssbbllkk command reads the ssyyssffss filesystem and uuddeevv ddbb to
       gather information. If the udev db is not available or llssbbllkk is
       compiled without udev support, then it tries to read LABELs, UUIDs and
       filesystem types from the block device. In this case root permissions
       are necessary.

       The command prints all block devices (except RAM disks) in a tree-like
       format by default. Use llssbbllkk ----hheellpp to get a list of all available
       columns.

       The default output, as well as the default output from options like
       ----ffss and ----ttooppoollooggyy, is subject to change. So whenever possible, you
       should avoid using default outputs in your scripts. Always explicitly
       define expected columns by using ----oouuttppuutt _c_o_l_u_m_n_s_-_l_i_s_t and ----lliisstt in
       environments where a stable output is required.

       Note that llssbbllkk might be executed in time when uuddeevv does not have all
       information about recently added or modified devices yet. In this case
       it is recommended to use uuddeevvaaddmm sseettttllee before llssbbllkk to synchronize
       with udev.

       The relationship between block devices and filesystems is not always
       one-to-one. The filesystem may use more block devices, or the same
       filesystem may be accessible by more paths. This is the reason why
       llssbbllkk provides MOUNTPOINT and MOUNTPOINTS (pl.) columns. The column
       MOUNTPOINT displays only one mount point (usually the last mounted
       instance of the filesystem), and the column MOUNTPOINTS displays by
       multi-line cell all mount points associated with the device.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --AA, ----nnooeemmppttyy
           Don’t print empty devices.

       --aa, ----aallll
           Disable all built-in filters and list all empty devices and RAM
           disk devices too.

       --bb, ----bbyytteess
           Print the sizes in bytes rather than in a human-readable format.

           By default, the unit, sizes are expressed in, is byte, and unit
           prefixes are in power of 2^10 (1024). Abbreviations of symbols are
           exhibited truncated in order to reach a better readability, by
           exhibiting alone the first letter of them; examples: "1 KiB" and "1
           MiB" are respectively exhibited as "1 K" and "1 M", then omitting
           on purpose the mention "iB", which is part of these abbreviations.

       --DD, ----ddiissccaarrdd
           Print information about the discarding capabilities (TRIM, UNMAP)
           for each device.

       --dd, ----nnooddeeppss
           Do not print holder devices or slaves. For example, llssbbllkk ----nnooddeeppss
           //ddeevv//ssddaa prints information about the sda device only.

       --EE, ----ddeedduupp _c_o_l_u_m_n
           Use _c_o_l_u_m_n as a de-duplication key to de-duplicate output tree. If
           the key is not available for the device, or the device is a
           partition and parental whole-disk device provides the same key than
           the device is always printed.

           The usual use case is to de-duplicate output on system multi-path
           devices, for example by --EE WWWWNN.

       --ee, ----eexxcclluuddee _l_i_s_t
           Exclude the devices specified by the comma-separated _l_i_s_t of major
           device numbers. Note that RAM disks (major=1) are excluded by
           default if ----aallll is not specified. The filter is applied to the
           top-level devices only. This may be confusing for ----lliisstt output
           format where hierarchy of the devices is not obvious.

       --ff, ----ffss
           Output info about filesystems. This option is equivalent to --oo
           NNAAMMEE,,FFSSTTYYPPEE,,FFSSVVEERR,,LLAABBEELL,,UUUUIIDD,,FFSSAAVVAAIILL,,FFSSUUSSEE%%,,MMOOUUNNTTPPOOIINNTTSS. The
           authoritative information about filesystems and raids is provided
           by the bbllkkiidd(8) command.

       --II, ----iinncclluuddee _l_i_s_t
           Include devices specified by the comma-separated _l_i_s_t of major
           device numbers. The filter is applied to the top-level devices
           only. This may be confusing for ----lliisstt output format where
           hierarchy of the devices is not obvious.

       --ii, ----aasscciiii
           Use ASCII characters for tree formatting.

       --JJ, ----jjssoonn
           Use JSON output format. It’s strongly recommended to use ----oouuttppuutt
           and also ----ttrreeee if necessary.

       --ll, ----lliisstt
           Produce output in the form of a list. The output does not provide
           information about relationships between devices and since version
           2.34 every device is printed only once if ----ppaaiirrss or ----rraaww not
           specified (the parsable outputs are maintained in backwardly
           compatible way).

       --MM, ----mmeerrggee
           Group parents of sub-trees to provide more readable output for
           RAIDs and Multi-path devices. The tree-like output is required.

       --mm, ----ppeerrmmss
           Output info about device owner, group and mode. This option is
           equivalent to --oo NNAAMMEE,,SSIIZZEE,,OOWWNNEERR,,GGRROOUUPP,,MMOODDEE.

       --nn, ----nnoohheeaaddiinnggss
           Do not print a header line.

       --oo, ----oouuttppuutt _l_i_s_t
           Specify which output columns to print. Use ----hheellpp to get a list of
           all supported columns. The columns may affect tree-like output. The
           default is to use tree for the column 'NAME' (see also ----ttrreeee).

           The default list of columns may be extended if _l_i_s_t is specified in
           the format _+_l_i_s_t (e.g., llssbbllkk --oo ++UUUUIIDD).

       --OO, ----oouuttppuutt--aallll
           Output all available columns.

       --PP, ----ppaaiirrss
           Produce output in the form of key="value" pairs. The output lines
           are still ordered by dependencies. All potentially unsafe value
           characters are hex-escaped (\x<code>). See also option ----sshheellll.

       --pp, ----ppaatthhss
           Print full device paths.

       --rr, ----rraaww
           Produce output in raw format. The output lines are still ordered by
           dependencies. All potentially unsafe characters are hex-escaped
           (\x<code>) in the NAME, KNAME, LABEL, PARTLABEL and MOUNTPOINT
           columns.

       --SS, ----ssccssii
           Output info about SCSI devices only. All partitions, slaves and
           holder devices are ignored.

       --ss, ----iinnvveerrssee
           Print dependencies in inverse order. If the ----lliisstt output is
           requested then the lines are still ordered by dependencies.

       --TT, ----ttrreeee[==_c_o_l_u_m_n]
           Force tree-like output format. If _c_o_l_u_m_n is specified, then a tree
           is printed in the column. The default is NAME column.

       --tt, ----ttooppoollooggyy
           Output info about block-device topology. This option is equivalent
           to

           --oo
           NNAAMMEE,,AALLIIGGNNMMEENNTT,,MMIINN--IIOO,,OOPPTT--IIOO,,PPHHYY--SSEECC,,LLOOGG--SSEECC,,RROOTTAA,,SSCCHHEEDD,,RRQQ--SSIIZZEE,,RRAA,,WWSSAAMMEE.

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help text and exit.

       --VV, ----vveerrssiioonn
           Print version and exit.

       --ww, ----wwiiddtthh _n_u_m_b_e_r
           Specifies output width as a number of characters. The default is
           the number of the terminal columns, and if not executed on a
           terminal, then output width is not restricted at all by default.
           This option also forces llssbbllkk to assume that terminal control
           characters and unsafe characters are not allowed. The expected
           use-case is for example when llssbbllkk is used by the wwaattcchh(1) command.

       --xx, ----ssoorrtt _c_o_l_u_m_n
           Sort output lines by _c_o_l_u_m_n. This option enables ----lliisstt output
           format by default. It is possible to use the option ----ttrreeee to force
           tree-like output and than the tree branches are sorted by the
           _c_o_l_u_m_n.

       --yy, ----sshheellll
           The column name will be modified to contain only characters allowed
           for shell variable identifiers, for example, MIN_IO and FSUSE_PCT
           instead of MIN-IO and FSUSE%. This is usable, for example, with
           ----ppaaiirrss. Note that this feature has been automatically enabled for
           ----ppaaiirrss in version 2.37, but due to compatibility issues, now it’s
           necessary to request this behavior by ----sshheellll.

       --zz, ----zzoonneedd
           Print the zone related information for each device.

       ----ssyyssrroooott _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y
           Gather data for a Linux instance other than the instance from which
           the llssbbllkk command is issued. The specified directory is the system
           root of the Linux instance to be inspected. The real device nodes
           in the target directory can be replaced by text files with udev
           attributes.

